"Locate the code path responsible for reading and writing the JSON file and identify where formatting is discarded.The full code path is small — `json_normalize.py` has exactly three stages. I've read the file; here's the precise anatomy with line references:\n\n## Code path\n\n**1. Read — `json_normalize.py:13-14`**\n```python\nwith open(args.file, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n```\n\n**2. Normalize (in memory) — `json_normalize.py:16-17`**\n```python\ndata[args.field] = data[args.field].strip().lower()\n```\n\n**3. Write — `json_normalize.py:19-21`**\n```python\nwith open(args.file, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, indent=4)\n    f.write(\"\\n\")\n```\n\n## Where formatting is discarded\n\nFormatting is lost in two compounding places:\n\n- **`line 14` — structural loss.** `json.load()` parses text into Python objects (`dict`/`list`/`str`). The object model holds *data only*: indentation width, tabs vs. spaces, separator spacing, trailing newline, and escaping choices are dropped by design. From this point on, the original bytes are unrecoverable from `data`.\n- **`lines 19-20` — active loss.** `open(..., \"w\")` truncates the user's file, then `json.dump(..., indent=4)` re-serializes from the in-memory object using the tool's hardcoded house style (4-space indent, default `\", \"` / `\": \"` separators). Line 21 then imposes its own trailing `\\n`.\n\nOne nuance from the earlier diff: key *order* survives, because Python 3.7+ dicts preserve insertion order and `json.load` reads in document order. So the observed damage is purely whitespace (2-space → 4-space). The same write path would also silently destroy tab indentation, custom separators, non-ASCII characters (`ensure_ascii=True` default), and float repr in files that use them — my sample just doesn't exercise those.\n\n## Implication for the fix\n\nBecause the information is destroyed at parse time, no write-side tweak can restore it. A semantics-preserving